<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wadi Hammamat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wadi Hammamat is a dry valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-desert">Eastern Desert</a>, on the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptos">Coptos</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast. From predynastic times it was a route to gold mines and the Red Sea, and its quarries supplied the dark greywacke prized for statues and sarcophagi. Hundreds of rock inscriptions record royal quarrying expeditions, especially of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wadi-hammamat.jpg" alt="Wadi Hammamat" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C. Meyer · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fig13_Guardpost_A4b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
